Dear Marc,

Thank you for all your help! I will try this method and see what comes out! Thank you for your suggestions about adding the effects for transients or trap-dependence, I will keep this in mind when the model doesn’t fit!

I have one other question about the grouping of my data. I would like to compare 1) recapture, 2) survival and 3) dispersal between two groups with the model shown below. I am able to make a group distinction for survival and recapture, however I do not know how to loop groups through the priors of dispersal (bold part of the script). Normally the output contains two values for sigma (one for the dispersal in the X direction and one value for the dispersal in the Y direction of the XY coordinates). So, when looping through groups I should have an X and Y value for each group as well.  Besides, what should change in the initial values?

Any help is appreciated!

Best regards,

Anne-Merel

# Model sCJS-N

sink("sCJSN.mod")
cat(

"

    model {
    # Priors and constraints
    for (i in 1:nind){
    for (t in f[i]:(n.occasions-1)){

    phi[i,t] <- mean.phi.g[group[i]]
    p[i,t] <- mean.p.g[group[i]]
    } #t
    } #i

    for (u in 1:g){
    mean.phi.g[u] ~ dunif(0, 1)               # Priors for group-specific survival
    mean.p.g[u] ~ dunif(0, 1)                 # Priors for group-specific recapture
    }

    for (i in 1:2){

    tau[i] <- pow(sigma[i],-2)
    sigma[i] ~ dunif(0,50)

    }

    # Likelihood
    for (i in 1:nind){
    # Define latent state at first capture
    z[i,f[i]] <- 1
    for (t in (f[i]+1):n.occasions){

    # State processes
    # Survival
    z[i,t] ~ dbern(phi[i,t-1] * z[i,t-1])
    # Dispersal
    G[i,t,1] ~ dnorm(G[i,t-1,1], tau[1])
    G[i,t,2] ~ dnorm(G[i,t-1,2], tau[2])

    # Observation process
    # Test whether the actual location is in- or outside the state-space
    r[i,t] <- step(G[i,t,1]-Xmin) * step(Xmax-G[i,t,1]) * step(G[i,t,2]-Ymin) * step(Ymax-G[i,t,2])
    Y[i,t] ~ dbern(p[i,t-1] * z[i,t] * r[i,t])
    } #t
    } #i
    }
    ",fill = TRUE)
sink()

# 2. Bundle data

jags.data.MN <- list(Y = dat.MN$Y.in, G = dat.MN$G.in, Xmin = 0, Xmax = 30, Ymin = 0, Ymax =

10, f = dat.MN$f, nind = nrow(dat.MN$Y.in), n.occasions = ncol(dat.MN$Y.in), g=length(unique(group)), group=group)

# 3. Initial values
inits.MN <- function(){list(mean.phi.g = runif(length(unique(group)),0,1), mean.p.g = runif(length(unique(group)),0,1), sigma = runif(2, 0.01,2), z = known.state.cjs(dat.MN$Y.in))}

# 4. Parameters monitored
parameters.MN <- c("mean.phi.g", "mean.p.g", "sigma")

Op vrijdag 10 mei 2019 16:17:03 UTC+2 schreef marc.kery:
> Dear Anne-Merel,
>

> capture-location is such an individual effect which you cannot fit in an analysis with the m-array aggregation (because individual identity is 'lost' in that aggregation). This may not be the best solution, but it seems like potentially useful to me to first ignore that covariate, do the m-array aggregation, fit a model and get a Bayesian p-value for a first, crude fit assessment. If you are lucky, this tells you that the model fits, so if you then do an analysis for the unaggregated data set, and with the location covariate added, then this model ought to fit be even better. If on the other hand the simpler model (without the location covariate) doesn't fit, then you may have to think about how to improve the model, e.g., by adding some random effects as a way to allow for 'overdispersion' or perhaps by adding effects for transients or trap-dependence (again, to diagnose the latter two, the tests in RELEASE can be valuable).
>
> Best regards  ----  Marc
>

